    {
      "dom_id": "question-82302",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194737/assets/2788f2795065599063c67d9ae7e9e6ee17cae4aa21198450faf7c09ad10765e1.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $6+3\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg.",
      "worked_solution": "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=6+3\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 6+3\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 3\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 3 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
